\section*{Exact van der Waerden numbers}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Superexponential van der Waerden growth}: The claimed lower bound is $W_r(k)>k^{ck\lfloor\log_2 r\rfloor}$ for one absolute positive $c$ once $k$ is large enough. In particular, for each fixed color count at least two, the $k$th root of $W_r(k)$ is unbounded.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Superexponential van der Waerden growth}: A cyclic-group coloring uses two circle-coordinate meshes related by an arithmetically chosen dilation. Counting anchored progression patterns gives a balanced-color versus affine-coordinate alternative uniformly in the growing dimension. A norm-dependent perturbation excludes the affine alternative, after which interval and product-color transfers give the stated bounds for all color counts.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Superexponential van der Waerden growth}: The theorem gives a uniform superexponential lower bound for all large progression lengths, but no exact values of W(r,k). It improves extremal bounds within the same family rather than completing the exact-value request.

This page records partial support. Complete resolution of the catalogue question is not established by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 160 describes Lean coverage for the uniform van der Waerden lower bound and its growth-limit consequences.

\textit{Superexponential van der Waerden growth}: The manuscript links \texttt{QuantitativeVanDerWaerden.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.QuantitativeVanDerWaerden.uniform\_lower\_bound}).

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

